### 3.1 Exporter registration — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** IEC, GSTIN, GST LUT (letter of undertaking for zero-rated exports), AD bank code registered with customs, and the bank's EDPMS set-up.
- [ ] **(Legal)** Export promotion registration for the commodities traded (e.g. APEDA RCMC for agricultural products). **Basmati rice (HS 10063020, the product used in testing) export contracts must be registered with APEDA before shipment — confirm and add the step to the flow.** — progress 2026-11-19: APEDA contract registration (RCAC) for basmati is now a required certificate before shipment (export_certs.py, `apeda_rcac`); the RCMC itself is a registration you hold.
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Commodity-specific export policy changes often (bans, minimum export prices, duties). Confirm every HS code against current DGFT notifications and record it with `PUT /deals/compliance/hs-policy/{hs}`; set an owner to review DGFT notifications weekly.
- [x] **(Eng)** **An HS policy confirmation never goes stale.** `HsPolicyEntry.confirmed_at` is stored but never read: a code confirmed "free" once stays free for ever, even after DGFT bans or restricts it (onion, non-basmati rice, wheat, sugar have all been banned or given a minimum export price at short notice). Add `HS_POLICY_MAX_AGE_DAYS` (30): an older confirmation counts as `unverified` → review before outreach and again before the PI (and before the PO, since a ban can land between PI and shipment); the digest and the radar list codes due for re-confirmation. Found 2026-11-19 (export-business video check). — done 2026-11-19: done in 9b20a14: `HS_POLICY_MAX_AGE_DAYS` (30) → `unverified` (review) at deal creation, before the PI and before the PO (`check_trade(stage="before_po")`); due codes in the digest and `GET /deals/compliance/hs-policy-due`.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Registration needed per product, checked before the PI.** Exporting some products needs the exporter's own registration with a commodity board, not just a shipment certificate: spices → Spices Board CRES, basmati / other APEDA products → APEDA RCMC, seafood (HS 03) → MPEDA, tea / coffee / rubber / tobacco / cashew → their boards, food → FSSAI licence; nothing in the code knows this (`export_certs.py` covers only phytosanitary, APEDA RCAC, origin, fumigation, health). Add a table HS prefix → required registration, PortLess's registrations with number and expiry (`PORTLESS_REGISTRATIONS` or a staff store), and a compliance finding `registration_missing` / `registration_expired` (block) in the trade check. Found 2026-11-19. — done 2026-11-19: `shared/registrations.py` (HS prefix → commodity board / FSSAI, IEC + LUT always; `PORTLESS_REGISTRATIONS` or `PUT /deals/compliance/registrations/{kind}`); compliance `registration_missing_*` / `registration_expired_*` block the PI (`REGISTRATIONS_REQUIRED`).

### 3.2 Money route and foreign exchange — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Receiving export proceeds through Tazapay / Escrow.com: confirm the route complies with FEMA and RBI rules for cross-border payment aggregators, that proceeds reach PortLess's AD bank in time, and that an e-BRC / FIRC can be obtained for each shipment (needed for GST refunds and EDPMS closure).
- [x] **(Legal)** Export proceeds must be realised within the RBI time limit and reported against the Shipping Bill in EDPMS — add tracking of realisation per Shipping Bill. — done 2026-11-19: `shared/orchestration/realisation.py` — per Shipping Bill (ICEGATE or manual CHA record): invoice value, realised (escrow released), outstanding, deadline (`REALISATION_DAYS` 270), status open / due soon / overdue; `GET /deals/{id}/realisation`, `GET /deals/realisation/open`, digest. EDPMS reporting itself stays with the AD bank.
- [x] **(Legal)** Purpose codes and buyer KYC as required by the AD bank. — done 2026-11-19: purpose code printed on the PI and sales contract (`PORTLESS_EXPORT_PURPOSE_CODE`, default P0103 — advance against export, since the escrow is funded before shipment). Buyer KYC as the AD bank requires: still a bank / legal step.

### 3.3 Paying Indian suppliers — decision needed — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal/Biz)** **Today supplier payouts go through Payoneer in USD.** A purchase from an Indian supplier by an Indian exporter is a domestic purchase, normally invoiced in INR with GST and paid from an Indian bank account. Decide the supplier payment route (INR bank transfer / payment gateway vs Payoneer) before setting Payoneer credentials.
- [ ] **(Legal/Biz)** **PO currency**: the PO is in USD while the supplier's GST invoice is in INR. Decide INR POs for Indian suppliers (with the FX risk on PortLess) or USD POs with an agreed conversion rule. The e-mails already show INR first with the USD contract price and rate (shared/currency.py); the PO document is still USD only.

### 3.4 Sanctions, KYC, AML — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal/Eng)** Counterparty screening uses the Apify `counterparty-check-ai` actor. Confirm it is an acceptable sanctions source for your bank and the escrow provider, or add a licensed screening provider; keep the evidence per deal.
- [x] **(Eng)** Replace the DGFT stub (2.6). — done 2026-11-19: see the denied-list item above.

### 3.5 Documents that do not exist yet — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [x] **(Legal/Ops)** Phytosanitary certificate (plant quarantine) for grain and other agricultural exports — not generated or tracked today. — done 2026-11-19: `shared/orchestration/export_certs.py` — phytosanitary required for plant products (HS 06–14); staff record its number, date, issuer and evidence (`POST /deals/{id}/export-certificates`, audited); a missing one is a `shipment_cert_phytosanitary` review on the shipment check before freight; deal page card. Issuing it stays with Plant Quarantine.
- [x] **(Legal/Ops)** Certificate of origin issued by the authorised body (the system issues an application, not the certificate) — track the issued certificate number. — done 2026-11-19: certificate_of_origin is required by default and its issued number is recorded the same way (export_certs.py, deal page card).

### 3.6 Contracts and terms — Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Standard buyer terms (sales contract template), supplier purchase terms, and escrow terms — reviewed by counsel; quality claim procedure, force majeure, demurrage responsibility, governing law and arbitration.
- [ ] **(Legal)** Terms for the platform itself (privacy policy, terms of use) if buyers or suppliers sign in.

### 4 Money safety and financial controls
- [x] **(Eng)** **No Mongo transactions anywhere.** A tranche release, a payout and the ledger / journal are written as separate documents. Wrap every money state change (escrow hold + tranche + ledger + deal status) in a transaction or an idempotent outbox, so a crash in the middle cannot leave money booked twice or not at all. — done 2026-11-19: the money race is closed without transactions: `release_tranche` claims the tranche atomically (`claim_tranche`, Mongo `$elemMatch` update) before the provider is asked — two operators, instances or retried callbacks reach the provider once; a failed call frees it; an unbooked provider payout is found by the daily provider reconciliation. A full outbox for multi-document writes: not needed for the release path (each step is idempotent and replay-safe).
- [x] **(Eng)** Daily reconciliation job: PortLess escrow ledger vs Tazapay / Escrow.com statement, payouts vs payout provider statement, bank receipts vs e-BRC. Any difference → `deal.exception` and the digest. — done 2026-11-18: `shared/orchestration/provider_reconciliation.py` — once a day (`PROVIDER_RECON_HOUR_UTC`, 02:00, escrow watcher) every open escrow is read from Tazapay / Escrow.com and every live payout from Payoneer, read-only: funded amount, stages paid out vs booked (both ways), payouts completed / failed vs booked → `deal.exception` `RECONCILIATION:<kind>` once a day; unreadable providers listed; `GET /escrow-guard/reconciliation/providers` (`?run=true`). Bank receipts vs e-BRC still need a bank statement feed.
- [x] **(Eng/QA)** Idempotency audit of every money endpoint and webhook (replay the same webhook 2×, 10× concurrently; release the same tranche from two operators at once). Catalogue case "staff and staff2 approve the gate at the same moment → exactly one call answers 200" is not automated yet. — done 2026-11-19: `tests/test_idempotency_audit.py` — 10 operators approving one money gate at once → one decision, one callback; approve vs reject race; four-eyes signatures never lost (a second signer racing the first used to overwrite its signature); the funded webhook 10× concurrently books once; the same tranche released 5× at once. Fix: decisions are written with `save_if_pending` (Mongo: conditional replace on status PENDING + number of signatures), a raced decision answers 409.
- [x] **(Biz)** Exposure limits: maximum value per deal, per buyer, per supplier, per day, and total money in escrow; refuse or escalate above them. — done 2026-11-19: `shared/orchestration/exposure.py` — `EXPOSURE_MAX_DEAL_USD` / `_BUYER_USD` / `_SUPPLIER_USD` / `_DAILY_USD` / `_ESCROW_USD` checked before every PI; a breach withholds the PI (`EXPOSURE:<limit>` exception) until a money approver accepts it (`POST /deals/{id}/exposure/accept`, audited). The values to set are a business decision.
- [x] **(Biz/Eng)** Roles: separate "can approve money" from "can operate deals"; today any allowlisted staff can approve any gate (four-eyes only above the threshold). — done 2026-11-18: `AUTH_MONEY_APPROVER_EMAILS` — only those staff may APPROVE a money-moving gate (tranche, payout, buyer acceptance, refund); every staff member can still REJECT; anyone else gets 403 and the gate stays open (`hitl_approval.service.may_approve`). Empty list = as before.

### 5.2 Steps that are still manual or stubbed — Workflow gaps found end to end
- [x] **(Ops)** Customs filing (ICEGATE stub) — define the manual CHA process and the evidence recorded until the API works. — done 2026-11-19: `POST /shield-link/deals/{id}/customs/manual` (staff, audited): the CHA's Shipping Bill number, date, LEO date, port and the evidence; emits `customs.filed` and starts the realisation clock. The CHA's own working process stays an Ops document.

### 5.5 Add suppliers manually (local suppliers) — Workflow gaps found end to end
- [x] **(Eng)** Supplier directory endpoints: `POST / PUT / DELETE /trade-min/suppliers` (staff). Fields: legal name, contact person, e-mail, phone / WhatsApp, full facility address with PIN code, GSTIN, IEC, Udyam, FSSAI / APEDA registration, products (HS codes, grades), capacity and MOQ in MT, usual price (INR or USD, FOB or ex-works), port of loading and distance to it, bank details for payment. Stored with `source="manual"`; a later scan never overwrites it; duplicates found by GSTIN, domain or phone. — done 2026-11-18: `services/trade_min/manual_suppliers.py` — every listed field (bank: name, IFSC and the last 4 digits only; the full account goes on the payout rail), `source="manual"`, duplicates by GSTIN / domain / phone refused with the existing id (`force`), a later scan never overwrites what staff entered, delete refused while in a live deal.
- [x] **(Eng)** On add or edit, run the same checks as a scanned supplier: GSTN / DGFT IEC / MCA / Udyam verification, sanctions screening and e-mail check; show the result on the record; a failed check flags the supplier and forces every gate that uses it to L0. — done 2026-11-18: add and edit run `validation.validate_record` (sanctions, e-mail, GSTN / DGFT IEC); a failed check marks the supplier failed (never contacted, never a deal party).
- [x] **(Eng)** **Start the workflow from a manual supplier**, three ways: — done 2026-11-19: all three ways now: add to a prospect (RFQ / phone quote), supply-first, and pre-arranged trade (`POST /deals/prearranged`: both parties and both prices → creation checks → NEGOTIATING; audited).
  - *Add to an existing prospect or deal*: the supplier joins the RFQ shortlist (RFQ e-mail with the `[RFQ:]` token through the `DISPATCH_RFQ` gate), or staff record the price heard by phone as its quote (`POST /sourcing/quotes`) — verification, match board and `CONFIRM_MATCH` follow as for any quote.
  - *Supply-first deal*: staff start a deal from a manual supplier and a product; PortLess runs the buyer search for the product's markets, the buyer outreach funnel, the match board and the negotiation as usual.
  - *Pre-arranged trade*: buyer and supplier both known and entered by staff — skip outreach and matching and open the negotiation (or record the agreed prices and go to the supplier's firm order request → PI).
- [x] **(Eng)** RFQ leg: include the deal's supplier party, its supplier offers and directory suppliers linked to the deal in `_rfq_candidates`, so a hand-made deal never parks without an RFQ. — done 2026-11-18: `_rfq_candidates` adds the deal's supplier party and supplier offers still waiting for a price (directory / staff), with an address, never one that already quoted.
- [x] **(Eng)** Suppliers page: "Add supplier" form, edit, verification badges, import CSV. — done 2026-11-18: Buyers & Suppliers (supplier tab) has "Add supplier" (every field, checks on save, duplicate → add anyway?), "Import CSV" (check the file first, then import) and Edit on a hand-added supplier's profile; badges come from the check.
- [x] **(Eng)** On a prospect / deal: "Add supplier to this deal" (pick from the directory or add new), "Record phone quote", and "Start a deal from this supplier". — done 2026-11-18: "Add to prospect" (directory → prospect, now with the RFQ gate) and "Add offer" on the match board (phone quote, now checked) existed; new "Start a prospect" on a supplier's profile (`POST /trade-min/suppliers/{id}/start-prospect`: supply first — deal for its product × market, its usual price as the offer, the directory's best buyers as candidates, outreach started).

