### 11.1 Capture money already owed to the exporter — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [x] **(Biz/Eng)** **Export incentives as income**: RoDTEP credits and duty drawback where the product qualifies — put them on the cost sheet as income per deal, track the claim and the credit received. Confirm eligibility for a merchant exporter per HS code with the CA. — done 2026-11-18: cost-sheet line `export_incentive` (negative, RoDTEP + drawback % × `INCENTIVE_FOB_SHARE` 0.9) once the CA has confirmed eligibility (`incentives.entry_for(...).eligible_confirmed`); never booked as a cost. Tracking the claim and the credit received: still open.
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **GST working capital**: export under LUT (no IGST outflow) and procure at the merchant-exporter concessional rate where eligible (3.3) — less cash locked per deal.
- [x] **(Biz/Eng)** **FX margin**: buy in INR, sell in USD; time the conversion, compare the AD bank rate with the escrow provider's rate, and add a small FX buffer to supplier offers. Optional forward cover for large deals. — done 2026-11-18: cost-sheet line `fx_buffer` (`FX_BUFFER_PCT` 0.5 % of the price) when the supplier is paid in INR; never booked as a cost. Comparing the AD bank and escrow-provider rates and forward cover: still open (decision 3.3).
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **RoSCTL for textiles, and the incentive declaration on the Shipping Bill** — `services/trade_min/incentives.py` knows only RoDTEP and drawback. Apparel (chapters 61, 62) and made-ups (63) earn RoSCTL instead of RoDTEP (Ministry of Textiles schedule, % of FOB with a cap per unit). Add `scheme` per HS entry (`rodtep` / `rosctl` / `drawback`; never RoDTEP and RoSCTL on the same item), import the rate table, and track `incentive_credits` per scheme. The claim is lost when the Shipping Bill does not carry the declaration, so the CHA pack (5.2) states which schemes to claim, and the Shipping Bill record keeps what was declared. An incentive counted on the cost sheet but not declared raises `INCENTIVE:not_declared` before LEO. Rate changes come from A.29 (Ministry of Textiles notifications, Appendix 4R). — done 2026-11-19: `ExportIncentive.rosctl_pct` / cap (HS 61–63 only, never with RoDTEP), `incentives.schemes` / `total_pct` in profit, matchmaking and the cost sheet; `incentive_credits` kind `rosctl`; the CHA pack lists the schemes to declare and the Shipping Bill record raises `INCENTIVE:not_declared` (cha.py). Rate changes from A.29 once built.

### 11.2 Cut costs on every shipment — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [x] **(Eng)** **Freight quotes from more than one source** (Maersk + forwarders) and book the cheapest that meets the date; today Maersk is booked by default. — done 2026-11-19: `services/shield_link/freight_compare.py`: every provider with real keys quoted before the BOOK_FREIGHT gate; the cheapest priced, non-stub quote within `FREIGHT_MAX_TRANSIT_DAYS` names the provider in the gate (quotes + lane checks beside it); `GET /shield-link/deals/{id}/freight-quotes`.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Container fill**: maximise MT per container (20 ft vs 40 ft, bag weight) in the cost sheet and the offer quantity. — done 2026-11-18: `trade_costs.container_plan`: cheapest 20' / 40' mix (`CONTAINER_20_MT`, `CONTAINER_40_MT_FACTOR`, `CONTAINER_40_COST_FACTOR`), fill % and the MT to offer the buyer when the last box is under 75 % full — on the cost sheet (`container_plan`, warning) and the console card. Report only: the freight line still assumes full containers (pricing part-empty boxes is a later decision).
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Use the freight APIs to pick the container that costs least per tonne without wasting space** — today `trade_costs.container_plan` chooses 20' vs 40' from fixed ratios (`CONTAINER_40_MT_FACTOR`, `CONTAINER_40_COST_FACTOR`) and only reports. Check what each freight API can answer: Maersk (Product Offers / rates per equipment type 20DRY, 40DRY, 40HC, reefer, with the max payload per box), Cogoport and Freightos (quotes per container type and LCL by volume / weight). Then: (1) quote every container option for the lane and the lot (product weight and volume per bag / MT from the catalogue, so a weight-limited cargo is not shipped in a box it cannot fill and a light cargo uses a 40' HC); (2) pick the mix with the lowest freight per tonne that keeps the boxes full (or LCL for a small lot); (3) price the deal on that quote and re-quote before booking; (4) offer the buyer the quantity that fills the last box when it lowers the price per tonne. Why: freight is the largest cost after the goods — a half-empty box or the wrong size is margin thrown away on every deal. Evidence: shared/orchestration/trade_costs.py container_plan (fixed ratios, report only); shared/connectors/maersk.py `quote` returns no per-equipment rates; freight_lanes.py takes one rate per lane and container type. — progress 2026-11-18: `shared/orchestration/container_choice.py` plans the cheapest 20DRY / 40DRY / 40HC (reefer 20RF / 40RH) mix per tonne from per-size lane quotes (`PUT /trade-min/freight-lanes` container types dry / dry40 / hc40 / reefer / reefer40) and the catalogue's stowage factor `m3_per_mt` (weight vs volume limit), reports fill and the MT that fill the last box; on the cost sheet (`container_choice`), `GET /deals/{id}/containers` and the console. Still to do: live per-equipment quotes from Maersk / Cogoport / Freightos (each API's container rates), LCL for small lots, pricing on the chosen plan and re-quote before booking. LCL (2026-11-19): small lots (≤ `LCL_MAX_MT` 12) are priced per freight tonne (W/M) from a lane quote `lcl` or the 20' rate × `LCL_PREMIUM`, and chosen when cheaper; `freight_lanes.refresh_all` asks the configured forwarder for every container size (20GP / 40GP / 40HC / reefers / LCL) when a deal's cost sheet is refreshed. — done 2026-11-19: freight sources are quoted per box type (20' / 40' / 40' HC, reefer per catalogue) and LCL for small lots; `freight_compare.cheapest_plan` picks the cheapest mix per tonne across providers for the BOOK_FREIGHT proposal.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Inspection cost**: compare inspector quotes per booking; offer the buyer a cheaper inspection plan when it is enough. — done 2026-11-19: `GET /deals/{id}/inspection-options`: enabled inspectors' prices for the booking, the current plan and lighter plans (always keeping the PSI) with savings, marked enough by lot size or a proven supplier; the buyer decides.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Escrow provider by cost**: pick the cheaper provider per deal size when the buyer has no preference; pass the escrow fee to the buyer as a line item where allowed. — provider choice done 2026-11-18: `ESCROW_PROVIDER_BY_COST=true` takes the enabled provider with the lowest fee (`<PROVIDER>_FEE_PCT`) when neither the buyer nor staff chose one (off by default). Showing the escrow fee to the buyer as a line item stays open.

### 11.3 Price better — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [x] **(Eng)** **Margin by corridor and buyer**: learn from accepted / countered indicative offers (outcomes already recorded) to set the target margin per HS code × market × buyer tier instead of one floor. — done 2026-11-18: `outcomes.corridor_margins`: per HS × market, ≥ `CORRIDOR_MARGIN_MIN_OFFERS` (5) indicative-offer answers: > 60 % accepted → target margin + `CORRIDOR_MARGIN_STEP_PTS` (2), < 20 % → − 2 (never below the floor); the match board's targets use it (`matchmaking.target_margin_pct(learned, market)`). Buyer tier (2026-11-18): key buyers (≥ `KEY_BUYER_MIN_SIGNED` 2 signed deals) get `KEY_BUYER_MARGIN_DISCOUNT_PTS` (1) off the target, never below the floor.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Value-added services priced explicitly**: private label packing, fumigation, palletising, extra inspections — a markup per service, shown in the offer. — done 2026-11-18: cost-sheet line `value_added_services`: `VAS_MARKUP_PCT` (15) on special packing and inspection phases beyond the first, so the buyer price carries a service charge, not only the cost.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Every Incoterm priced for what it really includes.** `landed_cost.basis()` folds CPT / CIP / DAP / DDP into CIF ("duty is the buyer's concern") and FAS / DPU are missing from `INCOTERMS`. A buyer asking DAP / DPU gets a price without destination terminal handling, unloading (DPU) or delivery to its warehouse; DDP would make PortLess pay import duty and VAT and act as importer of record in the buyer's country, which it cannot. Add destination-side cost lines (DTHC, destination haulage, unloading) for D-terms, price FAS (no loading) and FCA (handover at the container yard), and refuse or flag DDP: the negotiation offers DAP / CIF instead and a DDP request is held at L0 (`ddp_requested`). Found 2026-11-19 (Incoterms 2020 reel). — done 2026-11-19: `landed_cost`: all eleven rules (FAS, DPU added), D-term destination lines (DTHC, haulage, DPU unloading), FAS / FCA without loading, `ddp_refused`; a DDP draft or request is held at L0 (`ddp_requested`).
- [x] **(Eng/Legal)** **Incoterms 2020 wording on the documents.** The PI, sales contract and PO print the bare term ("CIF"); they should say "CIF Jebel Ali, Incoterms® 2020" (term + named place + version) so the risk and cost split is unambiguous. Insurance: CIF needs at least ICC (C) cover and CIP ICC (A) under Incoterms 2020 — the templates say 110 % but name no clause (`pi.html.j2`, `sales_contract.html.j2`, `insurance_declaration.html.j2`). For containers, consider FCA instead of FOB (ICC's recommendation: under FOB the risk stays with the seller between the container-yard handover and loading on board) — counsel to confirm. Found 2026-11-19. — done 2026-11-19: PI, PO, sales contract, CI and shipping instructions print "CIF Jebel Ali, Incoterms® 2020" (`incoterm_named`); CIF names Institute Cargo Clauses (C), CIP (A) (`icc_clause`) in the PI, sales contract and insurance declaration. FCA-for-containers stays with counsel.

### 11.4 Win more and repeat — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [x] **(Biz/Eng)** **Repeat orders and annual contracts**: a "reorder" button, reminders before the buyer's next usual order date, framework contracts with monthly lifts. — done 2026-11-18: `shared/orchestration/reorders.py` — `POST /deals/{id}/reorder` (a PAID deal → new prospect: same buyer + supplier, product, market, quantity, last agreed price as target; MATCHING so screening, compliance and the RFQ run); `GET /deals/reorders/due` + digest: buyers whose usual gap (median between their orders, else `REORDER_DEFAULT_DAYS` 90) puts the next order within `REORDER_REMIND_DAYS` (7); console Reorder button on PAID deals and a 'Reorders due' card on Prospects. Framework contracts with monthly lifts: still open.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Already-traded buyers and suppliers first**: a company PortLess has completed a deal with (PAID, no dispute or refund) is tried before a new one of the same product. Mark it on the directory record ("traded": deals completed, last deal, HS codes, dispute / refund count) and show it on Buyers & Suppliers and the radar. Use it in four places: (1) the scan adds traded companies for the HS code to the opportunity's buyers / suppliers even when the web search did not find them; (2) the outreach funnel contacts traded buyers first and the RFQ shortlist (`RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS`) always includes traded suppliers; (3) pair scoring and the match board rank a traded pair higher (today `outcomes.py` only adjusts the chance of closing); (4) before a negotiation opens with a new company, check whether a traded one can take the order (product, quantity, market, still passes validation) and ask it first. Never overrides a failed validation, a compliance hold or the margin floor; a past dispute or refund lowers the priority instead of raising it. — part done 2026-11-18: traded buyers are contacted first in the funnel and traded suppliers of the product always get the RFQ, first. Open: adding traded companies to a scan's opportunity, ranking a traded pair higher on the match board, asking a traded one before a new negotiation.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Buyer scoring for outreach**: focus the funnel on buyers most likely to close (reply rate, import history, fit score) — less outreach cost per deal. — done 2026-11-18: the funnel writes first to buyers PortLess already traded with, then verified ones, by their reply rate with PortLess, fit score and directory score (`outreach.rank_candidates`).
- [x] **(Eng)** Funnel analytics: scans → prospects → interested → matched → PI → paid, with time and cost per step. — done 2026-11-18: `GET /deals/funnel?days=` (opportunities → prospects → interested → matched → PI → paid: counts, conversion, median days, scan cost, e-mails, biggest drop); card on Money & Margin.

### 12 Buyer and supplier validation (Opportunity Radar)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Proof that a buyer actually imports this product.** Today a verified buyer is a real company with a working mailbox and a clean sanctions result, not a proven importer of the HS code. Needs import records (bill-of-lading data): paid Volza / ImportGenius, an Apify plan that can run the Store importer actors, or a new PortLess actor on public US customs bill-of-lading data (US-only coverage). See the decision below.
- [x] **(Eng)** Outreach and RFQ only to verified counterparties (switch, e.g. `OUTREACH_VERIFIED_ONLY` / `RFQ_VERIFIED_ONLY`): today a failed buyer / supplier is never contacted, but an unverified one still is. Decide first (decisions). — switches done 2026-11-18: `OUTREACH_VERIFIED_ONLY` and `RFQ_VERIFIED_ONLY` (both default off — today's behaviour); unverified buyers wait as pending, unverified suppliers are left out of the RFQ. Turning them on is the decision that remains.
- [x] **(Eng)** Sanctions "review" queue: counterparties whose screening returned `review` (possible match or a list unavailable) listed for staff, cleared or failed with a reason, audited like the compliance hold clearances; today they simply stay "unverified". — done 2026-11-18: `GET /trade-min/screening/review` (staff, oldest first) and `POST /trade-min/{buyers|suppliers}/{id}/screening-review` (clear / fail + reason) — the decision and what the screening said are kept on the record (`screening_reviews`); clear → verified when the e-mail is good, fail → failed (never contacted). Console: the queue card on Buyers & Suppliers.
- [x] **(Eng)** Re-check schedule: sanctions lists change — re-screen verified buyers / suppliers that are linked to open prospects or deals when their result passes `TRADE_MIN_VALIDATION_MAX_AGE_DAYS`, not only when a new scan finds them again. — done 2026-11-18: `services/trade_min/rescreen.py`, daily at 03:30 UTC (`scheduler_rescreen_cron`) and `POST /trade-min/screening/rescreen` (staff): verified companies of open prospects / deals whose check is older than the max age, oldest first, at most `TRADE_MIN_RESCREEN_MAX_PER_RUN` (20); now failed → `deal.exception` `SCREENING:rescreen_failed` on each open deal; review → the queue; no Apify token or no answer → the old result is kept.
- [x] **(Eng)** Confidence score counts the check: today `confidence_score` ignores validation (a corridor with 0 verified counterparties can score the same as one with 5). Add points for verified buyers / suppliers and show it in the radar's Confidence tooltip. — done 2026-11-18: up to `confidence_verified_max` (10) points — half for at least one verified buyer, half for at least one verified supplier (capped at 100); `confidence_basis` lists every part of the score and the radar shows it as the Confidence tooltip.
- [x] **(Eng)** Pair matching in the "All" view: mark or down-rank a pair with an unverified side; the Best Match column in "All" can currently name an unverified pair. — done 2026-11-18: every pair says whether both sides are verified (`verified`, `unverified_sides`); verified pairs rank and are recommended first, the best-match score comes from a verified pair whenever one exists (`best_match_verified`), and the radar marks unverified pairs.
- [x] **(Eng)** Foreign buyer company existence beyond the website: LEI (GLEIF, already returned by the screening actor), EU VIES for EU buyers (returned), and a company-register / domain-age check for other countries; show them on the badge. — done 2026-11-19: LEI (GLEIF) and EU VIES from the screening, CIN / Udyam / GST for Indian companies → `validation.existence` (the badge text), +5 on the directory score. Company register / domain age for other countries waits for A.6 / A.19.
- [x] **(Eng)** Console: show the supplier's `MatchResult.verification` + reasons on the prospect / deal page, and `BuyerCandidate.validation_status` in the outreach funnel list (backend fields exist, the deal pages do not show them yet). — done 2026-11-18: a Check column on the deal's supplier scoring table (reasons as tooltip) and a check chip on every buyer in the outreach funnel.
- [ ] **(Ops)** Directory hygiene with the check: run "Check now" on worldwide-search companies before they are used (they are never checked automatically — cost), and a bulk "check all unverified" action with a cost estimate first.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation: radar verified view hides unverified / failed, failed buyer never gets outreach, failed supplier never becomes the supplier party, reuse of a fresh result (no second actor run), no Apify token → nothing verified; browser case for the "Verified only / All" toggle and the directory filter.
