- [ ] **(Biz/Legal)** Founders' / shareholders' agreement (roles, equity, vesting, who signs and who approves money).
- [ ] **(Legal)** Trademark application for "PortLess" (name and logo) in the relevant classes (trading / logistics services).

### 14.2 Tax and trade registrations — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Udyam (MSME) registration — free; gives priority-sector lending, protection against late payment, and access to MSME export schemes.
- [ ] **(Legal)** FSSAI licence for food products — confirm with the CA / FSSAI which licence a merchant exporter of your products needs (central licence is typical for exporters).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Plant-quarantine registration for agri exports so the phytosanitary certificate can be applied for (also 3.5).
- [ ] **(Legal)** State registrations: Shops & Establishments licence for the office, professional tax (employer and directors).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Portals the business must use: DGFT, ICEGATE, GST, e-way bill (inland truck to port, also 3.5) and e-invoicing once turnover crosses the threshold.

### 14.3 Bank and payment accounts — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Pre-shipment / packing credit line with the bank (finances the supplier advance when a buyer's escrow is not yet released) and post-shipment credit if needed.
- [ ] **(Biz)** ECGC export credit insurance (policy and buyer limits) — cover against buyer default or country risk outside the escrow.
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Open marine cargo insurance policy with an insurer or broker (also 1.4 `PORTLESS_MARINE_POLICY`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Accounting software (Tally / Zoho Books) from day one, chart of accounts agreed with the CA (also 4).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Forward-contract (FX hedging) facility with the bank for large USD receipts (also 11.1).

### 14.4 Brand, website and contact details — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Business phone and WhatsApp Business number (local suppliers often answer only there), shown on the website and in the signature (`PORTLESS_PHONE`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** LinkedIn company page and a Google Business Profile for the office — buyers check both before replying to a first e-mail.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company profile / brochure PDF, letterhead, rubber stamp for signed export documents, visiting cards.
- [ ] **(Biz)** D-U-N-S number (Dun & Bradstreet, free) — many foreign buyers and their banks look a supplier up by it.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Fill the company profile settings once these exist: `PORTLESS_LEGAL_NAME`, `PORTLESS_WEBSITE`, `PORTLESS_CONTACT_EMAIL`, `PORTLESS_PHONE`, `PORTLESS_IEC`, `PORTLESS_GSTIN` (also 1.4).

### 14.5 Advisers and service providers — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company secretary (ROC filings) and a trade lawyer for the contracts (also 3.6).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Accounts with the providers the workflow uses: QIMA (inspection), Maersk (carrier) and one freight forwarder as a back-up (also 1.2, 2.3).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Courier account (DHL / FedEx / Aramex) for sending original documents (B/L, certificate of origin) to buyers' banks and samples to buyers.

### 14.6 Office, people and records — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Name board at the registered office (GST verification may check it) and a filing system for original documents (export documents must be kept for years, also 3.7).
- [ ] **(Biz)** People for the roles the platform expects: trade desk operator, money approver (second person for four-eyes), compliance officer (also 4, 8).

### 14.7 Product and market requirements — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Per destination: what the buyer's country needs before the goods arrive — e.g. US FDA food-facility registration and prior notice, Gulf halal certificate / SFDA (Saudi), EU pesticide residue limits (MRL) and certificate of analysis. Record it per product line in the catalogue.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **India Trade Portal as the source for destination requirements, refreshed on a schedule.** indiantradeportal.in (FIEO / Department of Commerce) gives per HS code × importing country: the MFN and preferential tariff (India's FTAs: UAE CEPA, Australia ECTA, ASEAN, Japan, Korea, Mauritius …), SPS and TBT measures, non-tariff measures, import licensing and the documents asked at the border. Today `import_requirements` is typed by hand and nothing re-checks it. Read it per catalogue product × market (connector or a new Apify actor — the site has no API) into `import_requirements` with source and date, refresh monthly, and on a change re-check open prospects / deals of that product × market (review finding `destination_requirement_changed`). Use the preferential rate on the radar's net gap and ask for a **preferential certificate of origin** (CoO e-platform) when an FTA lowers the buyer's duty — a selling point in the introduction. Found 2026-11-19 (export-business video: "check demand, import duty and regulations on the India Trade Portal before choosing the country").
- [x] **(Eng)** **Buyer's own import licence / permit before the PI.** Some product × country pairs need a permit held by the importer (e.g. rice / sugar import permits or quotas in several Asian and African markets, SFDA importer registration in Saudi Arabia, US FDA prior notice by the importer). Mark such `import_requirements` entries as `buyer_holds`; the negotiation's `missing_information` asks for the permit number and validity, and the PI waits for it (review finding `buyer_import_permit_missing`) — otherwise the goods can be refused at the destination port with the buyer's money already in escrow. Found 2026-11-19. — done 2026-11-19: catalogue `import_requirements` entries `buyer: …` → review finding `buyer_import_permit_missing` before the PI, asked in the negotiation's `missing_information`; `PUT /deals/{id}/buyer-permits` records number and validity.

### 14.8 Ongoing compliance calendar — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** One calendar with an owner for every recurring filing: monthly GST returns (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B), TDS returns, IEC annual update (April – June), LUT renewal (April), RCMC renewal, ROC annual filings (AOC-4, MGT-7), directors' KYC (DIR-3 KYC), income-tax return and tax audit, DSC renewal, insurance renewals.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Regulatory watch — DGFT and CBIC updates checked daily, not by memory.** A watcher (deal-watcher style, leader-locked) reads the DGFT notifications, public notices and trade notices pages and the CBIC customs (export duty) notifications, matches each new item against the catalogue's HS codes and product names, and for a hit: marks the HS policy entry "changed — re-confirm" (so the trade check reviews it), holds the PI / PO on open deals of that code, and pages Telegram + the digest with the notification link. Also reads DGFT's denied-entity list page and warns when a newer list than the loaded one is published (today the list is loaded by hand, `denied_list.py`). Found 2026-11-19 (user ask: follow the India Trade Portal / country regulations and their updates before every trade). — done 2026-11-19: `shared/orchestration/regulatory_watch.py`: daily from the deal watcher; a notice naming a catalogue product / HS code marks the HS confirmation for re-confirmation (8-digit → 6-digit), raises `REGULATORY:<hs>` on open deals, pages Telegram + digest; a newer DGFT denied list is reported.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Registration expiry in readiness and the digest.** Readiness checks that IEC / GSTIN / LUT ARN / AD code are *set*, never that they are *valid*: the IEC is deactivated if not updated every April – June, the LUT is per financial year (an export without a current LUT owes IGST), RCMCs expire after 5 years, FSSAI licences have their own term. Store each registration with its expiry; the digest warns 30 days before, readiness fails once one has lapsed, and a lapsed LUT / IEC / required RCMC blocks a new PI. Found 2026-11-19. — done 2026-11-19: each registration with its expiry; digest warns within `REGISTRATION_WARN_DAYS` (30), readiness `registrations_valid` fails in production once one lapsed, a lapsed IEC / LUT / required RCMC blocks the PI.

### 15.1 What is stored — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [x] **(Ops/Eng)** **Specification template per catalogue product**: each `ProductLine` lists the attributes that define a tradeable lot of that product, each with allowed values and a unit. Onion (HS 0703.10): form (fresh / dehydrated — a different HS code), variety and colour (Nashik red, pink, white, Bellary, Krishnapuram rose), bulb size in mm (45 to 55, 55+), crop (new / stored, month), quality tolerances (sprouting, rot, double bulbs, moisture), packing (5 / 10 / 25 / 50 kg mesh or jute bag), container (reefer or ventilated, MT per box). Without a template the product stays free text, as today. — done 2026-11-19: `ProductLine.attributes` (key, allowed values, unit, question, hard) — `shared/product_spec.py`; lines without one use base attributes that never block or ask. Filling the templates per product is the desk's part.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Buyer requirement on the directory record, per product**: what this buyer wants of this product, not only that it "buys onion": the template attributes plus quantity per order, how often, destination port, incoterm, target price and its basis, certificates and documents asked for (phytosanitary, fumigation, origin, organic), shelf life on arrival, label / private label, payment terms, season it buys in. — done 2026-11-19: `CounterpartyRecord.product_facts[hs6]` (`ProductFacts`): template attributes + quantity per order, port, incoterm, target price, certificates, delivery window, payment terms; `GET /trade-min/{buyers|suppliers}/{id}/products` with completeness and summary line.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Supplier offer on the directory record, per product**: what this supplier can actually supply: varieties, sizes and grades it packs, capacity per month, minimum order, months it has stock, packing options, price with its basis (ex-works / FOB) and date, certificates it holds (FSSAI, APEDA, GlobalG.A.P.), own packhouse or trader, plant location and nearest port. — done 2026-11-19: same structure for suppliers: grades, packing, capacity / month, MOQ, price + basis, port of loading, certificates, lead time.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Every fact carries its source and date**: company website, the buyer's / supplier's own e-mail, a completed deal, or staff. A fact nobody stated is shown as "not known", never filled from the HS code or a guess. A newer statement from the company replaces an older one; a staff entry is never overwritten by a scan. — done 2026-11-19: each `Fact` has value, source (scan / website / email / deal / staff), date, who, reference; precedence staff > deal > e-mail > website / scan, a scan only fills what is empty, unknown is absent.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Exact product code and name**: store the 8-digit ITC-HS code where known (the 6-digit code mixes different goods), one product name per catalogue line with its synonyms mapped to it (onion / shallot / "big onion"), and keep fresh, dried and processed forms as separate products. — done 2026-11-19: catalogue `hs8_codes`, `synonyms` (mapped by `catalogue.line_for_name`, used in searches) and `form` (fresh / dried / processed as separate lines); `line_for_hs8`.

### 15.2 How it is filled — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [x] **(Eng)** **From the company's own website at scan time**: the contact finder already opens the site; read the product page into the template (varieties, sizes, packing, certificates) for the top N companies, with the page as the source. — done 2026-11-19: template attributes and certificates named in the site text are stored as `website` facts on the directory record at scan time (`site_facts.py`).
- [x] **(Eng)** **From replies, and kept**: an outreach reply is already read into `BuyerRequirement` and a quote into `SupplierOffer`, but both live on the prospect and are lost to the directory when it closes. Copy them onto the company's record, and extend both readers to the template attributes (today: one free-text `grade` and `packaging`). — done 2026-11-19: a buyer reply's requirement (incl. template attributes read from allowed values) and a supplier quote are copied onto the company's record (source email) by `product_facts.record_buyer_requirement` / `record_supplier_offer`.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Ask for what is missing**: the buyer introduction and the RFQ list the product's unanswered template attributes as short questions (variety, size, packing, quantity per order, port), not a general "send your requirement"; the negotiation's `missing_information` uses the same list. — done 2026-11-19: introduction (item 6) and RFQ (item 7) list the product's unanswered template attributes; the negotiation brief puts them first (`outreach.spec_questions`).
- [x] **(Eng)** **From completed deals**: at PAID, write the PI / PO specification, quantity, packing and price onto both companies' records as their proven requirement / offer (feeds 11.4 "already-traded first"). — done 2026-11-19: at PAID the PI / PO facts (quantity shipped, price, packing, incoterm, ports) are written to both records as source `deal`, flag `traded`.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Staff edit**: a form on the company's page to enter or correct the requirement / offer per product (a phone call, a trade-fair meeting), audited. — done 2026-11-19: `PUT /trade-min/{buyers|suppliers}/{id}/products/{hs}` (staff, audited `product_facts_edited`); never overwritten by a reply or a scan. The console form is the UI repo's part.

### 15.3 How it is used — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [x] **(Eng)** **Match on the specification, not the HS code**: a buyer of 55 mm+ red onion in 25 kg bags is paired only with a supplier that packs it; a conflict on variety, size, form or packing is a hard filter and each matching attribute raises the pair score. Today a web-search buyer's product fit is a fixed 85 and only `grade` conflicts are checked. — done 2026-11-19: a conflict on a hard template attribute between `BuyerRequirement.spec` and `SupplierOffer.spec` blocks the pair (`specification mismatch`); each match raises the spec fit.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Buyers & Suppliers page**: one crisp line per product on each row ("Onion · red · 55 mm+ · 25 kg mesh · 2 × 40 ft reefer / month · CIF Jebel Ali"), how complete the requirement is (e.g. 6 of 9 known), a detail panel with each fact's source and date, and filters by product and attribute (variety, size, packing, port).
- [x] **(Eng)** **Radar and prospect pages** show the same line for each buyer and supplier, and what is still unknown before a deal starts; the pre-deal rescore lists the missing attributes under "still needed". — done 2026-11-19: backend part: the pre-deal rescore lists the template attributes neither company stated under `still_needed`; the summary line is on `…/products`. Showing it on the pages is the UI repo's part.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Carried into the deal**: the agreed specification fills the PI / PO description, the packing spec, the QC booking (inspection criteria, AQL, QIMA product ids) and the cost sheet (reefer vs dry box, MT per container) instead of being typed again at each step. — done 2026-11-19: the agreed specification (buyer's spec narrowed to the supplier's) is in the PI / PO product description and in the inspector's and carrier's notes (`product_spec.spec_text`).
- [x] **(Eng)** **Export policy per exact product**: the trade check reads the 8-digit code and form; onion is a product India has restricted before (export ban, minimum export price), so the current DGFT notification is checked before outreach and again before the PI (3.4, compliance seed). — done 2026-11-19: the trade check reads the catalogue's 8-digit code and form (`compliance.exact_product`); seed entries for fresh onion, dried vegetables, basmati / non-basmati rice; checked at deal creation and before the PI as before.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation: a requirement read from a reply lands on the directory record with its source; an unknown attribute is never invented; a variety / size / packing conflict blocks the pair; the outreach e-mail asks only the unanswered attributes; a staff entry survives the next scan; browser case for the product line, completeness and filters.
- [ ] **(Eng) P1** **Several items in one order (bill of quantities / BOQ)** — a deal carries one product, grade and quantity, but buyers often order a list: two grades of rice, three pack sizes, chilli + turmeric in one container. Add an itemised list (`order_lines`: product, 8-digit HS, spec, pack, quantity, unit, unit price, MOQ per line) through the RFQ, the quotes (a supplier may quote some lines only), the match board, the negotiation (price per line), PI / PO / CI / PL (one row per line plus totals), the QC booking (one QIMA product per line), the container plan over all lines, and the margin per line plus total. The floor holds on the total, and no line sells below its own landed cost.

### 15.4 How much the buyer buys — before and after contact — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
**Today (2026-11-18).** *After contact* the quantity is known: the introduction asks "Quantity (MT) and required delivery date", the reply is read into `BuyerRequirement.quantity_mt` (LLM gateway, regex fallback for MT / tons / containers / kg), the negotiation asks again while it is missing (`briefing.buyer_missing`), staff can set it (`PUT /deals/{id}/buyer-candidates/{cid}/requirement`), and the deal takes it as `quantity_mt` (else the radar's lot). *Before contact* nothing is known: only the US-importers actor returns a buyer's yearly volume, and the Apify plan cannot run it, so every web-search buyer has no volume, the pair score's **volume fit** (12–15 % of the weight) is always blank and the radar sizes every buyer at one container.
- [ ] **(Eng) P1** **Volume from import records — PortLess side** (US buyers: actor A.2; other markets: paid Volza / ImportGenius / Panjiva — decision 12): fill `BuyerSignal.annual_volume_mt` / `shipments_12m` / `last_import_date` (fields exist), so volume fit, reorder timing (`ranking.reorder_probability`, unused today) and yearly potential (16.3) work.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Quantities stated in public demand** — tenders, buy requests and RFQ boards state a quantity (21.3 `food-tenders-ai`, `buy-leads-ai`); carry it onto the buyer as a *stated* quantity with its source and date. — done 2026-11-19: `CounterpartyRecord.stated_quantity_mt` + `stated_quantity_source` + `stated_at` from every lead (tenders, buy requests, portal enquiries, mission lists), candidate `quantity_mt` on the prospect.
- [x] **(Eng) P2** **Order-size band when nothing is stated** — small / medium / large from company signals (employees, warehouses or branches on its site, number of product lines, the market's import value ÷ the number of importers found), shown as a band and labelled an estimate, never a quantity in an offer. — done 2026-11-19: `volume.order_size_band` (small / medium / large from site words, product lines, import value ÷ importers) on the directory record, labelled an estimate, never an offer quantity.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Show and use it**: "Buys ~X MT/yr · ships every ~N weeks (source, date)" on the directory card and profile, the radar's buyer list and the prospect desk; the radar's lot and the deal's default quantity use a stated or recorded quantity before the one-container default; pair volume fit stops being blank. — done 2026-11-19: company profile `volume.line` ("Buys ~480 MT/yr · ships every ~6 weeks (source)"); the radar's lot (and so the deal's default quantity) uses the median stated / recorded order of its buyers before one container.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Quantity read from a reply is kept and checked**: write it onto the directory record with its source (15.2 "from replies, and kept"); a reply in containers, bags or kg is converted with the product's container / bag size (catalogue, 16.3) and echoed back in the next e-mail for the buyer to confirm ("2 × 20' ≈ 36 MT — correct?"). — done 2026-11-19: containers (20' / 40' / HC / reefer, catalogue `container_mt`), bags × kg and kg converted (`extraction.quantity_detail`); the requirement keeps `quantity_stated` / `quantity_confirm` and the buyer brief asks "2 × 20' ≈ 40 MT — correct?" until a yes (`quantity_confirmed`). Writing it onto the directory record waits for 15.1.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Quantity against supply**: when the buyer's quantity exceeds the chosen supplier's stated capacity or MOQ blocks it, the desk says so and points to a second supplier (13.4 composition) instead of the match board only listing a blocker. — done 2026-11-19: `prospect_desk.supply_gap` — the chosen supplier's deliverable tonnes in the buyer's window (capacity × months, capped by its offered quantity) or its minimum vs the buyer's quantity; the desk row gets `supply_gap_mt` and a `second_supplier` move naming the other quoted suppliers that cover the rest.
- [ ] **(QA)** Tests: volume from a recorded import-record row reaches volume fit and the radar lot; a reply in containers is converted and confirmed; a band is never used as an offer quantity.

### 16.1 Hand-off from the radar to the prospect — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Eng)** **Ready-to-start checklist per opportunity** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/start_check.py`, `EnrichedOpportunity.readiness`): reachable buyers (usable e-mail, not flagged / failed; at least `OUTREACH_PARALLEL`), reachable suppliers, export policy, price checked, margin at the floor, duty verified, market news, deal already running, destination requirements — ok / warn / fail. The radar row shows one dot per check ("ready" / "N to check" / "N blocking"), the expanded row the list; "Start prospect" names every failing check and asks to confirm.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Carry the radar's numbers into the deal** — done 2026-11-17: the match seed passes `profit`, `lot_mt` and `flags`; the deal keeps them unchanged as `DealRecord.radar_estimate`, takes the radar's lot as its default `quantity_mt` and the radar's buyer price as its target when the scan had none (`buyer_price_source = radar:<basis>`, so the RFQ no longer falls back to "1.25 × cheapest FOB" when the radar had a price). Deal page: "Radar estimate → negotiated → realised" card.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Estimate vs outcome per opportunity** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/accuracy.py`, `GET /trade-min/profit/accuracy`): realised ÷ estimated margin per settled deal; with ≥ 3 settled deals of a product (else ≥ 5 overall) the radar multiplies its margin estimate by the median ratio (clamped 0.5–1.2) and shows "corrected: …". Learns as soon as deals settle — nothing to do until then.

### 16.2 What can stop a deal, shown before it starts — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Eng)** **Export policy on the radar** — done 2026-11-17: `compliance.check_hs()` + `check_destination()` at scan time (a prohibited product or embargoed market is dropped before any paid search, with the reason in the scan report) and on every list (never listed; "Export policy unconfirmed" flag, chance of closing unchanged, checklist amber). Staff confirm "free to export" from the expanded row (`PUT /deals/compliance/hs-policy/{hs}`, recorded with their e-mail).
- [ ] **(Ops/Legal)** Confirm the export policy of every catalogue HS code once against ITC-HS Schedule 2 / DGFT notifications (the radar's "Confirm free to export", or restricted / prohibited through the API) — today every code shows "unconfirmed" (all live opportunities). 8-digit codes and minimum export prices stay in 15.3.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Market news verdict on the radar** — done 2026-11-17: `market_check.run` at scan and rescore (cached `MARKET_CHECK_MAX_AGE_HOURS`), the list reads the cache only; avoid ×0.5 and caution ×0.85 on the chance of closing, "News: avoid / caution" flags, verdict + summary in the market section, checklist red / amber. Needs `TAVILY_API_KEY` for the best results (Google News RSS without it) and an LLM gateway for the verdict.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Destination import requirements on the row** — done 2026-11-17: catalogue `ProductLine.import_requirements` (per ISO-2 market or `*`), shown in the market section and as an amber check.
- [ ] **(Ops/Legal)** Fill `import_requirements` per catalogue product line (FDA registration / prior notice, halal / SFDA, EU MRL certificate, phytosanitary …) — the data half of 14.7.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Data year and staleness** — done 2026-11-17: `data_period` and `market_data_flags` kept from the export-market row; "Comtrade <year> · old" when older than two years, flag "Old prices", tip in "What's missing". Opportunities scanned before today show it after their next scan.

