### 22.1 Review board — Claude skills that review the whole app and feed this list

- [x] **(Eng/Biz) P1** **Review-board skills for PortLess**: build a set of Claude Code skills (in `.claude/skills/`, the way the `/actor-verifier` board reviews an Apify Actor) that review this app end to end from several roles and turn every finding into tasks in this `todo` file, so the gaps in the trading workflow are found and closed and the app makes more money per deal. — skills built 2026-11-18: `.claude/skills/portless-review-board/` (SKILL.md flow, eight member briefs with checklists and a "Learned" section, judge.md, `scripts/append_findings.py` — adds accepted findings to the right priority section, never twice, recounts the metrics; tested). The first full board run is the item below.
  - **One entry skill** (e.g. `/portless-review-board`) that runs the members in turn — or one member by name (`/portless-review-board trader`) — and a **judge** step that merges the findings, removes duplicates and ranks them by money at stake and risk.
  - **Board members, each its own skill / prompt with a clear brief and a checklist:**
    - **Commodity trader / trade desk** — sourcing, pricing, margins, negotiation, the prospect desk, missed revenue and deals that would stall.
    - **CFO / finance controller** — escrow, payouts, fees, FX, ledger and journal, reconciliation, realised vs estimated margin, where money leaks.
    - **Compliance & legal (export India / FEMA / sanctions)** — export policy, screening, documents, contracts, audit trail, what blocks a real trade.
    - **Operations / logistics** — QC, freight, customs, documents, supplier follow-up, delivery and acceptance.
    - **Buyer's view** and **supplier's view** — every e-mail and document they receive: clear, trustworthy, nothing missing or leaked.
    - **CEO / growth** — unit economics per deal, funnel conversion (scan → prospect → PI → paid), cost per deal (Apify, LLM, people), what to build next for more profit.
    - **Engineer / SRE / security** — reliability, data safety, background jobs, provider failures, secrets, tests and CI.
    - **Operator / UX** — the console as the trade desk uses it every day: clicks, missing screens, confusing states.
  - **What each member reads**: `CLAUDE.md`, this `todo`, the code (services, `shared/orchestration`, the console in `../portless-ui`), the test catalogue (`docs/testing/`), the readiness report and, where available, real deal data / automation run results — never secrets.
  - **Output**: one review report per run (`docs/reviews/<date>-board.md`: findings per member with evidence — file / line, screen, or e-mail — severity, money impact and the fix), and every accepted finding appended to `todo` in the right priority and area, with owner tag, a P0 / P1 / P2 and a one-line "why it makes or saves money"; nothing already in the list is added twice (match against open and done items). The to-do metrics are recounted at the end.
  - **Learning loop**: the lessons of each review go into the members' checklists so the next run starts from them; re-run the board after each batch of fixes and compare the reports.
  - Done when the board can be run with one command, each member produces specific findings with evidence, and the first full run's tasks are in this list.
- [x] **(Eng/Biz) P1** First full run of `/portless-review-board`: all eight members, the judge, the report in `docs/reviews/`, the accepted findings appended to this list; then fix, re-run and compare. — done 2026-11-18: report `docs/reviews/2026-11-18-board.md` (scorecard 6–7/10 per member, 16 findings, 14 accepted, 2 already listed); the accepted ones are under 22.2 in P0 / P1 / P2 (review-board B-01 … B-14); lessons added to the members' checklists. Re-run after fixing B-01 … B-14.

### 22.2 Review-board findings (2026-11-18) — P1
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **The audit log can lose entries when two instances write at the same time** — On DuplicateKeyError re-read the last entry and retry (bounded); and raise a deal.exception / Telegram alert when an audit write finally fails. Why: the audit trail a bank, auditor or the escrow provider asks for stays complete. Evidence: shared/audit_log.py:29 asyncio.Lock is per process; :86 insert_one({"_id": seq}) — two instances computing the same seq → DuplicateKeyError → entry logged as NOT recorded and dropped. (review-board B-03 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `audit_log.record` retries up to 6 times on a duplicate sequence (re-reads the last entry), then logs and pushes a Telegram alert "AUDIT LOG WRITE FAILED"; tested with a forced two-writer race.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **One operator alone can clear a possible sanctions match** — Clearing a review needs two different staff (like four-eyes money gates); failing needs one; both stay in the audit log. Why: one sanctions breach can freeze the escrow, the bank account and the company. Evidence: services/trade_min/screening_review.py:57 resolve() — a single staff decision turns sanctions "review" into "clear" (verified, contactable). (review-board B-04 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: clearing a sanctions review needs two different staff (`SCREENING_CLEAR_TWO_PERSON`, default on): the first clear is pending, the same person again → 409, a fail acts at once and closes a pending clear; console card shows "Confirm clear".
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **A stalled watcher is only reported in the next morning's digest** — Push a Telegram alert the moment a watcher goes stale (checked by every instance, sent once per stale period) and when the leader lock store cannot be reached. Why: a stuck shipment or a supplier waiting for its money is caught in minutes, not a day. Evidence: shared/heartbeat.py marks a watcher stale; only shared/orchestration/digest.py reports it (daily at DIGEST_HOUR_UTC); shared/leader_lock.py fails closed when Mongo is unreachable, so every instance skips its ticks. (review-board B-05 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `heartbeat.check_and_alert` + `run_monitor` (every minute, one instance under lock `heartbeat_monitor`): Telegram once when a watcher goes stale and once when it is back, and once when the leader-lock store stops answering (`leader_lock.last_error_at`).
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Fees recorded from statements never reach the ledger, the journal or the realised margin** — Feed recorded actual fees into the ledger (fees_total = actual when recorded) and post them to the journal (provider_fees / bank charges); realised margin then follows. Why: the margin per corridor that pricing learns from is the real one. Evidence: shared/orchestration/fee_check.py keeps recorded fees in collection deal_fees; services/escrow_guard/service.py realised margin and journal.py use fees_total = provider fee % × released and portless_costs only. (review-board B-06 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: the ledger's PortLess costs add recorded bank charges, payout fees and escrow fees above the estimate (basis `actual`); the journal posts them, so realised margin follows.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **A supplier on the do-not-contact list still gets RFQs, reconfirmations and best-and-final e-mails** — Apply the directory's do-not-contact and declined lists to every supplier e-mail path (RFQ dispatch, quote_reconfirm, best_and_final, send retries). Why: the mailbox's reputation is what makes every introduction land. Evidence: shared/orchestration/continuations.py _rfq_candidates / _hand_added_suppliers (no do_not_contact filter; only _traded_suppliers_first checks it); services/trade_min/relationships.py:297 blocked_contacts reads buyers only. (review-board B-07 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `shared/orchestration/contact_guard.py` — RFQ dispatch, quote reconfirmation and best and final skip a do-not-contact supplier (address or company domain, never a free-mail domain).
- [ ] **(Biz) P1** **Five money and risk switches are built but left off, waiting for a decision** — Decide each switch with a recommended setting (verified-only outreach / RFQ on once Apify screening is live; cheapest escrow when both providers are live; clean-up at 60 days) and set it in the deployed environment. Why: each switch either saves fees or keeps unverified counterparties out of the money path. Evidence: OUTREACH_VERIFIED_ONLY, RFQ_VERIFIED_ONLY (outreach.py, continuations.py), ESCROW_PROVIDER_BY_COST (escrow_guard/service.py), PROSPECT_CLEANUP_ENABLED (prospect_cleanup.py), SUPPLY_FIRST / manual suppliers without a buyer-side check — all default off or permissive. (review-board B-08 2026-11-18)
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **The public rate limit trusts X-Forwarded-For, so it can be bypassed** — Use the address the trusted proxy appended (the last hop, or a TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS setting) and fall back to request.client; never the caller-supplied first value. Why: the anonymous money doors stay protected against brute force and floods. Evidence: shared/auth/middleware.py:90 — client = first value of X-Forwarded-For, which any caller can set. (review-board B-09 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `rate_limit.client_address` uses `TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS` (default 0 = the TCP peer; N = the Nth entry from the right of X-Forwarded-For); a rotated header no longer gets past the limit.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Each module opens its own Mongo client and connection pool** — One shared client per process (shared/mongo.py get_client()), every module takes its collection from it. Why: a smaller Atlas tier carries the load and the backend does not fall over under a scan. Evidence: 29 modules call motor AsyncIOMotorClient(settings.mongo_uri) (e.g. shared/audit_log.py, heartbeat.py, leader_lock.py, fee_check.py, outcomes.py, pricebook.py). (review-board B-10 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `shared/mongo.py` (one client per URI, closed at shutdown) used by the 11 shared modules (audit log, heartbeat, leader lock, catalogue, autonomy policy, compliance, fees, market check, outcomes, supplier commitment, price book); the Kill Switch keeps its own client for its timeout. Service repositories: follow-up item below.
- [x] **(Eng) P2** **Service repositories still open their own Mongo clients** — Move the service repositories (`services/*/repository.py`, `init_repository()`) onto `shared.mongo.get_client`, keeping each test's mongomock client isolated and closing only at app shutdown. Why: one connection pool per instance instead of one per service. Evidence: `grep -rn AsyncIOMotorClient services/` after B-10. (review-board B-10 part 2 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: every service repository, the directory, the negotiation stores and the cache take `shared.mongo.get_client`; `close_repository` only drops its reference, `shared.mongo.close_all()` closes the pool at shutdown; tests start with no cached client (conftest). Only the Kill Switch, the automation store and the LLM tracker pool keep their own (tested).
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **New back-office features have no console screen** — Audit log + verify on System Health, watchers status there too, a fees form and list on the deal's Payments tab and Money & Margin, price alerts on the radar, clean-up preview on Prospects, Tally / Zoho download buttons. Why: the controls that protect margin are used every day instead of forgotten. Evidence: no calls in ../portless-ui/src/api/endpoints.ts for GET /hitl-approval/audit(/verify), POST/GET /escrow-guard/deals/{id}/fees and /fees/over, GET /health/watchers, GET /deals/price-alerts, GET/POST /deals/prospects/cleanup, the journal ?format=tally|zoho. (review-board B-11 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: System Health: watchers + audit log with "Verify chain"; deal Payments tab (staff): actual fees form + lines, exchange rates, journal downloads CSV / Tally / Zoho; Money & Margin: fees above the price + all-journals downloads; Radar: supplier price moves; Prospects: idle prospects with "Close idle prospects" (`portless-ui/src/api/backoffice.ts`, `components/backoffice/BackOfficeCards.tsx`).

### 22.2 Review-board findings (2026-11-18) — P1
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Traded suppliers take every RFQ slot, so the deal's own scored suppliers are never asked** — Reserve slots: at most 2 traded suppliers (RFQ_TRADED_MAX), the Match Engine's scored suppliers and the deal's own offers always get theirs, then rank the rest; log which supplier was dropped by the cap. Why: price competition on every RFQ — a new, cheaper supplier is asked instead of crowded out by old ones. Evidence: shared/orchestration/continuations.py:563-595 _traded_suppliers_first puts every directory supplier with deals_won > 0 for the HS code first, then :560 caps the list at RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS (5). Automation RUN-20261118-094219-69c2: UC-15 / TC-E2E-000 failed 'each supplier gets the RFQ … {S1: 0, S2: 0}' while the safety guard refused 5 real directory addresses (exactly the cap) — five 'traded' companies filled the list. (review-board B-15 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `continuations._cap_rfq`: traded suppliers take at most `RFQ_TRADED_MAX` (2) of the `RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS` slots, spare slots go back to them, the cap logs who was left out; a traded company already on the deal keeps the deal's own supplier id and contact. Automation UC-15 / TC-E2E-000 pass again.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Staff relays that release money are not in the audit log, and some do not record who did them** — Require the signed-in staff user on every relay route and write each one to audit_log with reason and evidence reference; show them in the deal timeline. Why: a disputed release can be defended, and a wrong one traced to a person, instead of written off. Evidence: services/shield_link/router.py:265 api_record_milestone and :276 api_record_loading take no user; services/escrow_guard/router.py:218 open-window and :232 clear-dispute take no user; audit_log.record is called only for pi.extended, escrow.release_relayed_by_staff, approvals, directory.merge, screening, compliance.hold_cleared, kill_switch, prospect.dropped — not for the QC result relay (shield_link/router.py:170), a DELIVERED milestone (opens the acceptance window → final tranche auto-release), the loading record (unlocks T2 + freight), dispute clearance, supplier order confirmation / PO ack / goods-ready date by phone, party contact changes. (review-board B-16 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `audit_log.staff_relay` on every money-moving staff relay — QC result, reschedule, goods-ready date, milestone, loading record, acceptance window opened, dispute raised / cleared, supplier order confirmation / next supplier / PO ack, party contact change; milestone, loading, open-window and clear-dispute now require a signed-in staff user.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Only one inspection is priced, however many the deal books** — One cost-sheet line per planned inspection phase (buyer band and walk-away follow); book each QC order in the ledger; charge a re-inspection after the supplier's own failure to the supplier (deducted from its payout, stated in the PO terms and the rework notice). Why: DUPRO + PSI + PEO is ~USD 900-1,200 of inspection priced as ~USD 350; re-inspections stop coming out of the margin. Evidence: shared/orchestration/trade_costs.py:275 one inspection per deal; DUPRO / PEO can be added by the buyer (inspection_plan.py, deal.inspection_types) and a failed QC books a re-inspection (playbooks.py:82); services/escrow_guard/service.py:1969 books the inspection line once unless the provider order reports a fee; the rework notice (notifications.py:629) does not charge the supplier. (review-board B-17 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: cost sheet prices one inspection per planned phase (`inspection_phases`; buyer band and walk-away follow); the ledger books each QC order; a re-inspection after the supplier's failed QC is a chargeback (`shared/orchestration/chargebacks.py`, `SUPPLIER_REINSPECTION_CHARGE`) that lowers what the supplier is owed (ledger, payout cap — a payout above the net entitlement is refused —, PAID rule, payout gates), is posted in the journal and stated in the rework notice.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Phone / WhatsApp relays and radar data entry have no console screen** — Deal page 'Supplier said (by phone)' panel (confirm order, next supplier, PO acknowledged, goods-ready date, facility address — call note through askReason); QC card: record result, poll, reschedule; Payments: provider confirmed; packing, cost sheet and market check cards; radar settings: freight lane quotes and the accuracy table. Why: a deal never waits a day for a step the supplier already confirmed by phone. Evidence: extends todo line 422 (backend relays). No call in ../portless-ui/src for POST /deals/{id}/supplier-commitment/confirm, /next, /po-ack (gates the QC booking), POST /shield-link/deals/{id}/qc-readiness, POST /shield-link/qc/{ref}/status (SGS / BV results), /qc/poll, /qc/{tpo}/reschedule, POST /escrow-guard/escrows/{id}/tranches/{tid}/provider-confirmed, GET/PUT /deals/{id}/packing, GET /deals/{id}/cost-sheet, /market-check, PUT /trade-min/freight-lanes, GET /trade-min/profit/accuracy, POST /trade-min/screening/rescreen. (review-board B-18 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: `portless-ui/src/components/deal/RelayPanels.tsx`: Logistics tab 'Supplier said (by phone / WhatsApp)' (confirmed the order, declined → next supplier, PO acknowledged, goods-ready date, facility address) and packing; QC rows: record result, check provider now, reschedule; escrow tranches: 'Provider paid out' for a SUBMITTED stage; Payments: cost sheet + market check; Radar: freight per lane (add quote) and radar accuracy. The reason dialog gained date and select fields.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **The evidence pack leaves out compliance decisions, the audit trail and screening reviews** — Add sections: compliance (checks, holds, clearances with reason and person), audit (entries for the deal and its parties + chain verify result), screening reviews of both parties, fees and FX, cost sheet. Why: a frozen payment, bank query or customs audit is answered with one file instead of weeks. Evidence: shared/orchestration/evidence.py has deal, parties, PI/PO, documents, orders, ledger, journal, approvals, exceptions, emails, timeline and party screening status — no compliance_checks / hold clearances, no audit_log entries for the deal, no directory screening_reviews (who cleared a possible sanctions match), no fees / FX, no cost sheet. (review-board B-19 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: the evidence pack (JSON + HTML) adds compliance checks, open holds and clearances, the HS policy as confirmed, the audit entries of the deal and its parties with the chain check, screening reviews, fees, FX and chargebacks.
- [x] **(Eng) P1** **Buyer and supplier e-mails make claims that are not always true** — Say 'verified' only when gst_verified and iec_active are True; name the bookable inspector; drop the reorder sentence; 'FOB Indian port or ex-works' for suppliers; add a test that scans every template for these phrases. Why: no dispute lost on a sentence PortLess wrote itself. Evidence: shared/orchestration/matchmaking.py:609 indicative offer: 'supplied by a GST- and IEC-verified Indian manufacturer' even when the pair's supplier is unverified (matchmaking.py:324-327) and 'the pre-shipment inspection by the agency you choose' (also trade_costs.py:356) while only QIMA is bookable (qc_providers.py:959); shared/company_profile.py:113 'Buyers who receive a good first shipment reorder through us' (a track record not yet earned) and :110 'you deliver FOB Indian port' while ex-works suppliers are collected by PortLess. (review-board B-20 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: 'verified' only when the pair's supplier passed GST and IEC (`PairScore.supplier_verified`); the inspector named is the one that can be booked (`qc_providers.inspector_phrase`) in the indicative offer, the price basis and the negotiation fallback; the reorder sentence is gone; suppliers read 'FOB Indian port, or we collect ex-works'; `tests/test_true_claims.py` scans for the phrases.
- [x] **(QA) P1** **Eight automation cases fail and the test directory carries real e-mails and state between runs** — Fix each case (seeded payout / settlement legs, catalogue wording for the provider release, add the org-user persona); after the live scan the worker rewrites directory e-mails to .invalid and clears do-not-contact / traded state per case; target 0 failed before the nightly run is wired. Why: the money paths stay proven end to end, and no test run can ever address a real supplier. Evidence: RUN-20261118-094219-69c2: 675 passed, 8 failed, 186 not automated, 135 browser. Failing: UC-15 + TC-E2E-000 (B-15), TC-PAY-002 (payouts_total 0, expected 18000), TC-PAY-014 (no balance payout gate after a partial payout), TC-LED-001 (deal realized_margin_usd None at settlement), TC-REL-016 (T4 SUBMITTED, catalogue expects RELEASED — provider release flow), TC-OUT-013 (C1 declined: a do-not-contact flag left in the directory by an earlier case), TC-RSK-075 (persona 'org-user' missing in services/automation/engine.py:101). All five money / outreach cases fail the same way on commit ec5920a, so they predate today's changes. The live scan keeps real supplier e-mails in trade_counterparties; the safety guard refused 5 of them. (review-board B-21 2026-11-18) — done 2026-11-18: all 8 fixed and re-run green: payout step opens the CLEARED payout gate; seeded settled deals carry their realised margin; TC-PAY-014 / TC-REL-016 catalogue wording follows the capped payout and the provider release; persona `org-user`; candidate test addresses unique per case; the worker turns every directory e-mail into a .invalid address and lifts leftover do-not-contact flags (`seed.neutralise_directory`); UC-15 closes earlier runs' open deals of its product; `AUTOMATION_LOG_LEVEL` for the worker log.

