## Apify actors — built outside this repo

PortLess's own actors live in the agent-pool repos on the `rich_minds` account. Nothing in this section is done in this repository. Each actor below says **why** PortLess needs it, the **source**, the **input** and the **row** it must return (field names exactly as written — the PortLess normaliser is written against them), the **rules** it must keep, when it is **done**, and the **PortLess side** (a separate item in its own area). "New" = a new actor via `/actor-pipeline`; "change" = `/actor-implement` on the existing clone, then `/actor-test` → `/actor-publish` → `/actor-deploy`.

**Contract every PortLess actor keeps** (so the connector, the cost guards and the tests need nothing special per actor):
- **Plan.** Runs on the `rich_minds` plan: no third-party Store actors and no Apify model proxy (both refused today). Plain HTTP + parsing (Crawlee Cheerio; Playwright only where the page needs JavaScript). Deterministic rules decide every field; `enableAi` stays an optional extra that defaults to false.
- **Common input.** `sourceMode` (`live` | `list` | `dataset`), `enableAi` (false), `maxQualified` (row cap), `maxDiscoveryChargeUsd` (spend cap per run), `dedupeAcrossRuns` (PortLess sends false), `goalDescription` (optional). Countries are ISO-2 codes. HS codes are strings without dots, 6 or 8 digits, and an 8-digit code also matches its 6-digit heading. Dates are ISO 8601.
- **Every row** carries `source` (short id), `sourceUrl`, `fetchedAt` (UTC) and `flags[]` (quality warnings). An unknown value is `null`, never a guess. Free text is trimmed to 2,000 characters. A stable `rowId` (hash of source + natural key) lets PortLess upsert.
- **OUTPUT record** `{ "ok": bool, "rows": n, "refused": [{ "source", "reason" }], "queries": [...], "costUsd": n, "dataPeriod"?: "YYYY-MM" }`. A source that blocked, changed layout or timed out gives `ok: false` with the source listed in `refused`. PortLess then shows `source_refused`, never a silent empty result. Parse failures are listed, not swallowed.
- **Law and manners.** Public pages only. Keep each site's terms and robots rules. Polite rate: at most one request per second per host unless the site allows more, with backoff on 429 / 503. No captcha solving. No personal social-media scraping (no LinkedIn profiles). No sign-in walls, except PortLess's own account where the terms allow it. Personal data is kept to business contact fields (DPDP, 3.7).
- **Pricing.** Pay-per-event per row (`/actor-pricing`); the first run of a mode is measured with PortLess's scan cost meter (16.3) before it is turned on for every scan. PortLess's daily budgets (`APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY`, `APIFY_MAX_USD_PER_DAY`) and result cache (`APIFY_CACHE_HOURS_<KEY>`) apply.
- **Done when** (every actor): unit tests on saved pages for each source, input schema ⇄ RunConfig ⇄ dataset schema in sync, README listing every input and row field below, a live smoke run on the platform, and 5–10 real rows saved for PortLess as `tests/fixtures/apify/<slug>.json` (the normaliser tests run on them).
- **PortLess side** (each its own item): actor key + `default_slug` + `quote()` + normaliser in `shared/connectors/apify.py`, a row in `GET /trade-min/sources`, a readiness warning while not configured, and tests on the recorded rows.

### Lists of buyers and suppliers (21.3, 18.1, 12)

- [ ] **(Apify)** A.1 **`india-export-stats-ai` (new)** — India's own export statistics from DGCI&S TradeStat.
  - **Why**: the freshest record of what India already sells to a market, at 8 digits and monthly. Comtrade is annual and often two years old. It feeds the merge rule (17.1) and the radar's demand and price prior.
  - **Source**: tradestat.commerce.gov.in → Export → Commodity-wise / Country-wise (web form, no API, no download link). Monthly and annual views; value in USD and INR, quantity with unit.
  - **Input**: `hsCodes[]` (ITC-HS 2–8 digit), `countries[]` (ISO-2, empty = all), `fromMonth` / `toMonth` (YYYY-MM, default the last 13 published months), `frequency` (`monthly` | `annual`).
  - **Row**: `hsCode` (8 digits as published), `hsDescription`, `country` (ISO-2), `countryName`, `period` (YYYY-MM or YYYY), `valueUsd`, `valueInr`, `quantity`, `unit` (as published: KGS / TON / NOS …), `quantityMt` (converted when the unit is a weight, else null), `unitValueUsdPerMt`, `provisional` (bool).
  - **Rules**: map TradeStat country names to ISO-2 through a fixed table, and flag `country_unmapped` when the name is not in it. Never sum provisional and final months silently. Cache a month (data is published monthly, around mid-month).
  - **Done when**: 090422 × DE / AE / US for the last 13 months matches the site by hand on three sampled cells.
  - **PortLess side**: 17.1 "India's own export statistics — PortLess side".
- [ ] **(Apify) P1** A.2 **US import records (new, only if decision 12 picks our own actor)** — who in the US actually imports the product, from public bill-of-lading data.
  - **Why**: a buyer that has imported the product in the last 12 months is far more likely to close. It also gives real lot sizes and frequency for the volume band (15.4).
  - **Source**: public US Customs manifest data (CBP AMS releases as republished on public sites). Decision 12 chooses between this actor and a paid data provider (ImportGenius / Panjiva / ImportYeti-style). Check each site's terms first.
  - **Input**: `products[]` (keywords), `hsCodes[]`, `originCountries[]` (default IN), `months` (default 12), `excludeForwarders` (default true), `maxConsignees`.
  - **Row**: `consigneeName`, `consigneeAddress`, `consigneeCountry` (US), `website` (when published), `shipments12m`, `totalMt12m`, `typicalShipmentMt`, `frequencyDays`, `lastShipmentDate`, `origins[]` (ISO-2 with share), `topShippers[]` (names), `products[]` (bill-of-lading descriptions), `isForwarder` (bool).
  - **Rules**: NVOCC / forwarder consignees are flagged and dropped by default. Weights are converted to MT. Shipper names are kept as published; they are the competing origin's exporters.
  - **Done when**: a known US importer of basmati appears with its last shipment date.
  - **PortLess side**: 15.4 volume band, 21.3 directory buyers (source `us_customs`), the radar's buyer quality.
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.3 **`company-contact-finder-ai` — contact persons (change)** — the named buyer, not info@.
  - **Why**: introductions to a named purchasing / import manager get more replies, and the person's role tells the negotiation agent who decides.
  - **Source**: the company's own team / about / contact / impressum pages (already crawled).
  - **Input**: add `extractPeople` (default true) and `preferRoles[]` (PortLess sends `purchasing`, `import`, `export`, `sales`, `general`; already accepted).
  - **Row**: extend `people[]` with objects `{ name, title, roleGroup (purchasing | import | export | sales | management | other), email, phone, sourceUrl }`, plus `linkedinCompanyUrl` (the URL only, as linked from the site — the actor never opens LinkedIn).
  - **Rules**: only people the company itself publishes. Never guess an e-mail pattern (no "firstname.lastname@" invention). If more than 10 people are found, keep the first 10, those matching `preferRoles` first.
  - **Done when**: 20 sampled buyer sites give ≥ 1 named person on the sites that publish one, and none is invented.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.2 "contact persons" (people on the directory record, introduction addressed to the person).
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.4 **`company-contact-finder-ai` — product range (change)** — does this company really deal in our product?
  - **Why**: today a supplier whose site names another product is only caught by a keyword rule (`product_mismatch`). A product-range classification makes pair matching and the radar's supplier trust honest.
  - **Input**: `products[]` (the corridor's product names and local names) and `hsCodes[]`.
  - **Row**: `productRange { listsProduct (bool), matchedTerms[], otherProducts[] (top 10), privateLabel (bool|null), packSizes[] (e.g. "1 kg", "25 kg bag"), organic (bool|null), certifications[] (FSSAI, BRCGS, ISO 22000, HACCP, Organic, Halal, Kosher — as named on the site), evidenceUrls[] }`.
  - **Rules**: a decision comes only from words on the site, recorded in `matchedTerms` / `evidenceUrls`. A certification named on the site is a claim, not a check (it is labelled `claimed`).
  - **Done when**: 20 sampled supplier sites give no false `listsProduct=true` on a site that does not show the product.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.2 "product range" (directory fit score, `product_mismatch` replaced by the actor's evidence, certification claims shown next to the A.34 check).
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.5 **`trade-fair-exhibitors-ai` (new)** — buyers and suppliers from trade-fair exhibitor lists.
  - **Why**: exhibitors at food fairs are real, active companies that already trade internationally, and their product categories are given.
  - **Source**: public exhibitor directories. Buyers: Gulfood (Dubai), Anuga (Cologne), SIAL (Paris), Biofach, Fruit Logistica, Seafood Expo Global, Sial Middle East. Suppliers: World Food India, AAHAR, India International Seafood Show, World Spice Congress, IIJS-type commodity fairs.
  - **Input**: `fairs[]` (ids from a fixed list in the README), `edition` (year, default latest), `categories[]` (fair category names or keywords), `countries[]` (exhibitor country filter), `role` (`buyer` | `supplier`, used only to label rows).
  - **Row**: `companyName`, `country`, `city`, `website`, `hall`, `stand`, `categories[]`, `productsText`, `fair`, `edition`, `exhibitorUrl`, `email` / `phone` (only when the directory publishes them).
  - **Rules**: one adapter per fair, behind a fixed list. A fair whose directory needs a sign-in is listed in `refused`. Refresh per fair edition (cache a year per fair × edition).
  - **Done when**: 2 buyer fairs and 2 supplier fairs return ≥ 50 exhibitors each, filtered by category.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.3 "trade-fair exhibitors" (directory source `trade_fair:<fair>`, then the normal contact finder + check).
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.6 **`counterparty-check-ai` — company age, size and activity (change)** — is the company alive and old enough?
  - **Why**: compliance blocks a company under one year old (`COMPLIANCE_MIN_COMPANY_AGE_YEARS`), and today age is known only from an Indian CIN. A dead site is a strong scam or closure signal.
  - **Input**: `website` / `domain` per item (PortLess already sends name, country, VAT, LEI and registration number).
  - **Row**: add `domainCreated` (RDAP; WHOIS fallback), `domainAgeYears`, `siteLastUpdated` (newest date found: copyright year, news / blog date, sitemap `lastmod`), `employeeRange` (as published on the site or company page, e.g. "11-50"), `activitySignals[]`.
  - **Rules**: use RDAP first (structured, allowed). Never treat a privacy-redacted WHOIS record as young. Flag `site_stale` when nothing is newer than 2 years.
  - **Done when**: a 2-month-old domain is flagged, and a long-standing company is not.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.2 "company age" (compliance age check for foreign buyers, trust score).
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.7 **`counterparty-check-ai` — reputation and adverse media (change)** — fraud, scam or default stories about the company.
  - **Why**: a buyer named in "scam" / "fraud" / "did not pay" stories should never get a PI. A supplier with complaints should not get the RFQ.
  - **Source**: Google News RSS / GDELT for the company name + country with risk words (fraud, scam, default, arrested, sanctions, insolvency, ED / CBI raid for India), public scam-report forums, and the public review rating where one exists (Google / Trustpilot summary only).
  - **Row**: add `adverseMedia[] { title, url, date, source, riskWords[], nameMatchScore }`, `adverseMediaVerdict` (`none` | `possible` | `likely`), `reviewRating`, `reviewCount`.
  - **Rules**: only hits with name match ≥ `minNameScore` and the right country count. A hit older than 5 years is labelled `old`. The verdict never turns a sanctions `clear` into `block`; it can only add `review`.
  - **Done when**: a known fraud case is found, and a same-name company in another country is not matched.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.2 "reputation" (screening review queue shows the hits, compliance `review` finding `adverse_media`).
- [ ] **(Apify)** A.8 **`company-contact-finder-ai` — search diagnostics (change)** — why a search found nothing.
  - **Why**: today an empty corridor cannot be told apart from a bad query. The scan report (16) needs a reason per query.
  - **Row / OUTPUT**: OUTPUT `queries[] { query, country, language, googleDomain, resultsSeen, sitesOpened, kept, dropped: { directory, marketplace, association, noContact, wrongCountry, duplicate, blocked } }`.
  - **Done when**: one run with 3 queries shows the counts adding up to `resultsSeen`.
  - **PortLess side**: scan report drop reasons per product × market (16.4 console).
- [ ] **(Apify) P1** A.9 **`india-exporter-registries-ai` (new)** — Indian suppliers from the official registered-exporter lists.
  - **Why**: a supplier registered with its commodity board is a real exporter. Some products cannot be exported without the registration (Spices Board CRES, APEDA RCMC for basmati, MPEDA for seafood).
  - **Source**: public lists of the Spices Board (registered exporters / CRES holders), APEDA (registered exporters, basmati RCAC holders), MPEDA (registered exporters), Tea Board, Coffee Board, Cashew EPC, and FIEO's member directory.
  - **Input**: `registries[]` (`spices_board`, `apeda`, `mpeda`, `tea_board`, `coffee_board`, `cepc`, `fieo`), `products[]` (keywords), `states[]` (Indian states, optional).
  - **Row**: `companyName`, `registry`, `registrationNumber`, `registrationType` (CRES / RCMC / …), `validUntil` (when published), `products[]`, `address`, `city`, `state`, `pin`, `email`, `phone`, `website`.
  - **Rules**: when a registry publishes PDFs, parse them and record the PDF date in `publishedOn`. When a registry has no product filter, filter by keyword on the products field.
  - **Done when**: the Spices Board and APEDA lists both return ≥ 100 rows for pepper / basmati.
  - **PortLess side**: 21.3 "registered exporters" (directory suppliers, registration recorded as verified fact).
- [ ] **(Apify) P1** A.10 **`india-b2b-suppliers-ai` (new; fills the never-built `india-supplier-finder-ai` slot)** — suppliers with a listed price from Indian B2B marketplaces.
  - **Why**: gives the supplier-side price estimate before any RFQ (price book prior) and many small suppliers the search misses. No paid IndiaMART keys are needed.
  - **Source**: public seller and product pages on IndiaMART, TradeIndia and ExportersIndia (no sign-in, no buy-lead pages).
  - **Input**: `products[]`, `hsCodes[]`, `states[]`, `maxPerSite`, `sites[]` (default all three).
  - **Row**: `companyName`, `city`, `state`, `productTitle`, `price`, `priceUnit` (per kg / quintal / tonne / bag), `priceUsdPerMt` (converted at the run's USD/INR, recorded in `fxRate`), `moq`, `moqUnit`, `gstVerified` (badge), `gstin` (when shown), `yearsInBusiness`, `responseRate`, `listingUrl`, `site`.
  - **Rules**: a price shown as "Get latest price" is null, not 0. A price range keeps both ends. Phone numbers that are marketplace call-tracking numbers are flagged `masked_phone`.
  - **Done when**: 3 products give ≥ 30 sellers each, with prices normalised to USD/MT.
  - **PortLess side**: connector slot `INDIA_SUPPLIER_FINDER` (exists, unused) → normaliser on these fields, prices into the price book as `listed` (lower weight than a quote).
- [ ] **(Apify) P2** A.11 **`apmc-traders-ai` (new)** — licensed traders at the main mandis.
